      "side": 0,
      "message": "The long term goal here would be that the `wakeup-irqs` property gets converted to a `wakeup-gpios`, matching the upstream convention.\n\nI see two options:\n1. Leave the Intel platform alone, using the legacy TCPC interrupt model.\n2. Add the `wakeup-gpios` property, but continue to allow `wakeup-irqs`.\n\n```\nhibernate-wake-pins {\n  wakeup-irqs \u003d \u003c\u0026int_ac_present ...\u003e;\n  wakeup-gpios \u003d \n    \u003c\u0026gpio4 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW\u003e, /* port 0 */\n    \u003c\u0026gpio6 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW\u003e, /* Port 2 */\n    \u003c\u0026gpio6 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW\u003e; /* port 3 */\n  \n```\n\nYou would need to add another `DT_FOREACH_PROP_ELEM()` for the `wakeup-gpios` property.  The IRQ callback has already been setup by the TCPC driver, so your helper macro only needs to call `gpio_pin_configure_dt()` and `gpio_pin_interrupt_configure()`.",
